  "full_response": "A load balancer's primary role in a web application architecture is to distribute traffic across multiple servers to ensure that no single server becomes overwhelmed by too many requests. This helps to maintain optimal performance and availability of the web. Load balancers effectively manage the flow of network traffic by directing a portion of incoming client requests to different servers in a pool based on various algorithms and policies. Some common algorithms are round-robin, least connections, and IP hash.\n\n\nRound-robin cyclically distributes incoming requests to servers in a sequential order. Least connections sends new requests to the server with the lowest number of concurrent connections, assuming all connections are of equal load. IP hash directs clients to a server by hashing its IP address and associating it with a server, offering predictability in server distribution for a given client.\n\n\nLoad balancers also provide additional features such as authenticating users, caching, SSL offloading, health checking to ensure the availability of servers, and sometimes even SEO optimizations if the architecture is structured as a reverse proxy. Moreover, they aid in the scaling process, which means they can facilitate both scaling out (adding more servers in the pool) and scaling up (upgrading existing servers). This allows web applications to handle varying workloads and maintain high availability, even during traffic spikes or server failures.",
